  <p><strong>How should a brand sample and approve the inner liner of 2-in-1 running shorts?</strong></p>

  <h2>Decide how much lining the runner needs</h2>
  <p>A brief liner has less material around the thigh. A fitted short liner adds coverage, but introduces a second leg opening and additional points of contact. Tell the supplier whether the short is intended for distance running, gym training or casual wear. Avoid specifying a compression claim unless it is supported for the finished garment.</p>

  <h2>Fit the shell and liner as one garment</h2>
  <p>Ask for separate liner and shell measurements. Review liner inseam, waist attachment and leg opening alongside shell rise, side slit and outer inseam. Put the sample through repeated high steps and jogging. Check that the liner does not pull the shell out of shape or become visible at the slit. Review the smallest and largest planned sizes where feasible.</p>

  <h2>Write a sample request the factory can answer</h2>
  <p>Send a sketch of both layers, fabric preferences for each, target inseams, the size range and whether the liner should be visible at full stride. Ask for a marked-up sample if an attachment or seam position changes. After the agreed wash and dry cycle, remeasure both layers and photograph any new asymmetry or exposure before signing off.</p>

  <p>Industry reference: <a href="https://www.minghangapparel.com/shorts-manufacturer/2-in-1-shorts-manufacturer/">Minghang Apparel’s 2-in-1 shorts manufacturing overview</a>.</p>
